From: Eric Berquist <eric.berquist@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] plumed: add enableMpi, so parallel PLUMED runs are possible

plumed is built with `buildInputs = [ blas ]` and nothing else, so the
library has no MPI support at all. Any PLUMED run on more than one rank
therefore fails, and a GROMACS patched with it -- `gromacs.override
{ enablePlumed = true; }` -- fails in whichever way the rank count picks:

    $ gmx mdrun -s prod.tpr -plumed plumed.dat -ntmpi 2
    Program:     gmx mdrun, version 2024.2-plumed_2.10.1
    MPI rank:    0 (out of 2)
    Unknown exception: (exception type: N4PLMD6Plumed14ExceptionErrorE)
    (tools/Communicator.cpp:110) void PLMD::Communicator::Set_comm(...)
    you are trying to use an MPI function, but PLUMED has been compiled
    without MPI support

Four rank counts, same 15270-atom system, same tpr and plumed.dat:

    (default: 128 thread-MPI ranks)  exit 139  double free or corruption
                                               (fasttop)
    -ntmpi 1                         exit 0    HILLS, COLVAR, .gro written
    -ntmpi 2                         exit 1    the message above
    -ntmpi 4                         exit 1    the message above

Only the serial run works. The default is the one that matters for a user
who does not know to cap it: a thread-MPI gmx takes a rank per core, and
the missing MPI support surfaces there as a crash rather than as the
diagnostic, because 128 ranks throw at once.

PLUMED is unusual in how it is told about MPI, and `--enable-mpi` is not
it. From user-doc/Installation.md:

    The flag `--enable-mpi` is perfectly valid but is not needed here.
    Autoconf will check if a code containing MPI calls can be compiled,
    and if so it will enable it. [...] Thus the correct way to enable MPI
    is to pass to ./configure the name of a C++ compiler that implements
    MPI using the CXX option.

PLUMED's own CI does exactly that: .github/workflows/linuxWF.yml sets
CC=mpicc and CXX=mpic++ for every `-mpi-` variant and passes no MPI flag
to configure. So this sets the two compilers, and puts mpi in
nativeBuildInputs so the wrappers are on PATH to be found.

The serial branch gains `--disable-mpi`, which is not redundant. MPI
search is on by default and searches `mpic++`/`mpicxx` before the plain
compilers, so without it a serial plumed stays serial only for as long as
no MPI wrapper appears in the build closure.

Left off by default, so nothing that builds today changes. A parallel
GROMACS then wants both halves:

    gromacs.override {
      enablePlumed = true;
      enableMpi = true;
      plumed = plumed.override { enableMpi = true; };
    }

Found through aiida-gromacs, whose three metadynamics tests drive
`gmx mdrun -plumed` and currently have to force `-ntmpi 1` to run at all.

---
--- a/pkgs/by-name/pl/plumed/package.nix
+++ b/pkgs/by-name/pl/plumed/package.nix
@@ -3,6 +3,8 @@
   lib,
   fetchFromGitHub,
   blas,
+  mpi,
+  enableMpi ? false,
 }:
 
 assert !blas.isILP64;
@@ -22,7 +24,30 @@
     patchShebangs .
   '';
 
-  buildInputs = [ blas ];
+  # `mpic++` has to be on PATH for configure to use it, hence mpi in
+  # nativeBuildInputs as well as buildInputs.
+  nativeBuildInputs = lib.optionals enableMpi [ mpi ];
+
+  buildInputs = [ blas ] ++ lib.optionals enableMpi [ mpi ];
+
+  # PLUMED does not take `--enable-mpi` as the switch, unlike most autoconf
+  # projects.  user-doc/Installation.md: "The flag `--enable-mpi` is perfectly
+  # valid but is not needed here [...] the correct way to enable MPI is to pass
+  # to ./configure the name of a C++ compiler that implements MPI using the CXX
+  # option."  PLUMED's own CI agrees -- .github/workflows/linuxWF.yml sets
+  # CC=mpicc and CXX=mpic++ for its `-mpi-` variants and passes no flag.
+  #
+  # `--disable-mpi` on the other branch is not redundant: MPI search is on by
+  # default and looks for `mpic++`/`mpicxx` first, so a serial build stays
+  # serial only as long as no MPI wrapper wanders into the build closure.
+  configureFlags =
+    if enableMpi then
+      [
+        "CC=mpicc"
+        "CXX=mpic++"
+      ]
+    else
+      [ "--disable-mpi" ];
 
   enableParallelBuilding = true;
 
